WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.315
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:09.176
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit three: Environmental Ethics. Today you will reflect on past moral choices with modal perfects.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:18.993
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:23.247
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:25.386
Anthropocentrism.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:30.484
Anthropocentrism means the view that humans are the most important beings.

8
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:34.604
Anthropocentrism treats forests merely as resources.

9
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:38.159
Your turn. Say: Anthropocentrism.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.477
Anthropocentrism.

11
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:44.671
Intrinsic value.

12
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:49.192
Intrinsic value means worth in itself, not because it is useful.

13
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:53.211
Many ethicists argue that species have intrinsic value.

14
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:56.643
Your turn. Say: Intrinsic value.

15
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.024
Intrinsic value.

16
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:03.318
Ecological footprint.

17
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:09.184
Ecological footprint means the amount of nature needed to support a person or society.

18
00:01:09.783 --> 00:01:13.048
Rich countries have a far larger ecological footprint.

19
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.836
Your turn. Say: Ecological footprint.

20
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.293
Ecological footprint.

21
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.910
Intergenerational justice.

22
00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:29.447
Intergenerational justice means fairness between present and future generations.

23
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.443
Climate debt raises questions of intergenerational justice.

24
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.759
Your turn. Say: Intergenerational justice.

25
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.646
Intergenerational justice.

26
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:44.489
Stewardship.

27
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:49.294
Stewardship means responsible care of something entrusted to us.

28
00:01:49.883 --> 00:01:52.730
Buddhist teaching encourages stewardship of nature.

29
00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:55.818
Your turn. Say: Stewardship.

30
00:01:58.667 --> 00:01:59.784
Stewardship.

31
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.422
Sustainability ethics.

32
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:08.295
Sustainability ethics means moral principles for meeting needs without harming the future.

33
00:02:08.883 --> 00:02:11.939
Sustainability ethics questions endless growth.

34
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.154
Your turn. Say: Sustainability ethics.

35
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.953
Sustainability ethics.

36
00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:23.194
Conservation ethic.

37
00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:27.831
Conservation ethic means a moral commitment to protecting nature.

38
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:31.775
A conservation ethic protected the Cardamom forests.

39
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:35.802
Your turn. Say: Conservation ethic.

40
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:40.353
Conservation ethic.

41
00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:42.605
Moral obligation.

42
00:02:43.617 --> 00:02:46.673
Moral obligation means a duty based on what is right.

43
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:50.432
We have a moral obligation to future generations.

44
00:02:51.017 --> 00:02:54.284
Your turn. Say: Moral obligation.

45
00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:58.668
Moral obligation.

46
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:00.836
Habitat loss.

47
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.672
Habitat loss means the destruction of places where species live.

48
00:03:06.283 --> 00:03:09.003
Habitat loss threatens the Irrawaddy dolphin.

49
00:03:09.617 --> 00:03:12.501
Your turn. Say: Habitat loss.

50
00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:16.751
Habitat loss.

51
00:03:17.517 --> 00:03:18.634
Mitigation.

52
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.752
Mitigation means action to reduce the severity of harm.

53
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.104
Climate mitigation requires cutting emissions.

54
00:03:26.717 --> 00:03:29.067
Your turn. Say: Mitigation.

55
00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:32.958
Mitigation.

56
00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:37.510
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:44.601
Visal, deforestation in Cambodia accelerated over the last twenty years. Who is responsible?

58
00:03:45.717 --> 00:03:54.313
Many actors. But we should have adopted a stronger conservation ethic sooner, before so much habitat loss had happened.

59
00:03:55.417 --> 00:03:57.021
Could it really have been avoided?

60
00:03:58.117 --> 00:04:06.261
Much of it could have been. Governments might have protected forests if they had valued their intrinsic worth, not just their timber.

61
00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.280
What is our moral obligation now?

62
00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:20.652
Mitigation and stewardship. Earlier generations ought to have acted, but blaming them won't help; future generations depend on what we do today.

63
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.358
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:24.217 --> 00:04:27.772
Grammar focus: Modal perfect for ethical reflection.

65
00:04:28.072 --> 00:04:29.270
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:29.983 --> 00:04:33.423
We should have adopted a stronger conservation ethic sooner.

67
00:04:34.317 --> 00:04:36.479
Much of the damage could have been avoided.

68
00:04:37.383 --> 00:04:40.497
Earlier generations ought to have acted on the warnings.

69
00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:51.014
Modal perfects judge the past: should / ought to have = a duty that was not fulfilled; could have = a possibility that was missed; might have = a possible but uncertain outcome.

70
00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:55.331
Be careful! Don't say: We should adopted it sooner.

71
00:04:57.267 --> 00:04:59.847
Say: We should have adopted it sooner.

72
00:05:00.147 --> 00:05:02.831
Modal perfect: should have + p.p.

73
00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:06.204
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.582
Who Speaks for Nature?.

75
00:05:08.882 --> 00:05:14.190
Environmental ethics asks whether nature has value only because it serves human needs.

76
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:22.169
Anthropocentric views have dominated modern economics, encouraging the exploitation of forests and rivers as mere resources.

77
00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:28.870
Critics argue that we should have recognised the intrinsic value of ecosystems long before the current crisis.

78
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:36.092
Intergenerational justice adds a further dimension: people not yet born cannot defend their own interests.

79
00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:42.594
From this perspective, stewardship is not charity but a moral obligation we owe to the future.

80
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:47.760
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:48.617 --> 00:05:50.674
The forest blank protected years ago.

82
00:05:50.974 --> 00:05:54.697
A, should have been. B, should have. C, should been.

83
00:05:58.233 --> 00:06:00.140
The answer is A: should have been.

84
00:06:01.283 --> 00:06:04.142
Worth in itself, not because it is useful, is blank.

85
00:06:04.442 --> 00:06:09.732
A, ecological footprint. B, intrinsic value. C, mitigation.

86
00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:15.882
The answer is B: intrinsic value.

87
00:06:17.017 --> 00:06:20.177
Fairness between present and future generations is blank.

88
00:06:20.477 --> 00:06:26.151
A, anthropocentrism. B, stewardship. C, intergenerational justice.

89
00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.011
The answer is C: intergenerational justice.

90
00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.962
Choose the sentence that expresses a missed possibility.

91
00:06:37.262 --> 00:06:41.752
A, We must protect it.. B, We could have protected it.. C, We protect it..

92
00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.298
The answer is B: We could have protected it..

93
00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:53.017
According to the reading, stewardship is described as…

94
00:06:53.317 --> 00:06:58.166
A, a moral obligation. B, charity. C, an economic resource.

95
00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.361
The answer is A: a moral obligation.

96
00:07:05.567 --> 00:07:08.683
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.966
Do humans have a moral obligation to protect the environment?

98
00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.215
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:31.515 --> 00:08:09.458
In my view, humans clearly have a moral obligation to protect the environment, both for other species and future generations. Having the greatest power over ecosystems, we carry the greatest responsibility. Cambodia's forests, for example, should have been protected more strongly, since their loss now affects rainfall and wildlife. Some argue that poor communities must put economic survival first. That is understandable, so I believe protection must go hand in hand with fair alternatives for local people.

100
00:08:09.758 --> 00:08:11.503
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:12.417 --> 00:08:17.449
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and modal perfect for ethical reflection.

102
00:08:17.749 --> 00:08:24.117
Today's value is environmental responsibility. Small green actions at home help the whole planet.

103
00:08:24.417 --> 00:08:27.162
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

104
00:08:27.462 --> 00:08:30.346
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

